    "final_text": "The CSV library is written and all 272 tests pass. I ran the full suite once at the end through `dx run-gate --name full-gate`.\n\n**What's there**\n- **`src/csv.js`** is the CommonJS entry point. It exports `parse`, `createParser`, `stringify` and `CsvError`.\n- **Supporting modules:** `src/parser.js` is one scanner shared by `parse` and `createParser`, so chunked input can't drift from whole-text parsing. The other modules are `header.js`, `stringify.js`, `options.js` and `errors.js`.\n- **`package.json`** has `npm test` running `node --test test/*.test.js`. There are no dependencies, not even dev ones.\n- **Tests** are in five files: `parse`, `errors`, `stream`, `stringify` and `roundtrip`.\n- **`README.md`** covers the API, error positions, and the choices I made where the spec was silent.\n\n**How it was checked**\n- **Chunking:** the streaming tests compare `createParser` with `parse` at every single split point, every pair of split points, one character per chunk, and 1,500 random chunkings. They compare both records and `CsvError` line/column.\n- **Round trip:** 3,000 seeded random cases test `parse(stringify(rows, opts), opts)` across 7 delimiters, both line endings, and with and without `skipEmptyLines`.\n- **The tests can fail:** I broke 13 behaviours one at a time in a scratch copy, including CRLF split across chunks, BOM column offset, escaped quotes and the field-count check. Every one made the suite fail.\n- **Speed:** 15M characters parse in about 130 ms, whether in one string or in 4 KiB chunks. 400k one-character writes finish in about 20 ms, so each `write` only reads the new chunk.\n\n**A bug the tests caught:** if the header ended with a delimiter (`x,,`), a duplicate empty name was reported at the wrong column. It's fixed.\n\n**Decisions where the spec was silent** (all listed in the README):\n- **BOM:** a leading BOM takes up no column, so in `\\uFEFF\"a\"x` the `x` is at column 4.\n- **Multi-line records:** a field-count error reports the line where the record starts.\n- **Duplicate header names** are checked when each field ends. So `a,a,\"b` reports the duplicate, not the unclosed quote.\n- **Leading BOM in output:** `stringify` also quotes the first field if the output would otherwise start with U+FEFF. This goes beyond the spec's quoting rules, but without it `parse` would drop that character and the round trip would fail.\n- **`stringify` header:** it must be a non-empty array of unique strings. With a header and no rows, it writes just the header line.\n- **Options:** `header` and `skipEmptyLines` must be booleans in `parse`. Unknown keys like `eol` are ignored, so one options object works for both `parse` and `stringify`.\n- **Delimiter:** a \"single character\" means one UTF-16 code unit, so an emoji delimiter is rejected.\n\n**Git:** the work is on branch `feat/csv-parser` as four commits. There's no remote, so nothing was pushed. The repo's commit hooks required conventional commit messages and the Dex co-author trailer instead of the Claude one, so I followed them. I left `CLAUDE.md` untracked. `CLAUDE.md` also asks for a `humanizer` skill pass on docs, but that skill isn't installed, so I edited the README by hand. `dx ps` shows nothing left running from this session.",
